It was perhaps apt that, just as the rain lashed down on Los Angeles on Friday, the final day of the American Film Market , the biggest deal announced from the event happened to be for a movie called &#8220;Wet.&#8221;

Earlier in the week, Lionsgate nabbed North American and U.K. rights to Alma Har'el's film, boasting an all-star cast of Connor Storrie, Rebecca Ferguson, Odessa A'Zion, Javier Bardem, Rosalia and Lukas Haas and being sold by Fifth Season and 193, A Legendary Company. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories ‘Godzilla Minus Zero’ Stars Relive Hollywood Ovation and Fan Chants for Yamazaki Takashi at Tokyo Festival From 'Dil Se' to 'Ramayana': How Indian Cinema Shattered the U.K., Ireland Box Office Glass Ceiling

But rain wasn't the biggest external factor at play as the AFM &#x2014;&#xA0;now into its second year at Fairmont Century City&#xA0;&#x2014;&#xA0;closed it doors. Friday also happened to be the same day as the FAA's nationwide plane grounding, sparking chaos around the country. Numerous attendees who had been hoping to get home for the weekend saw their flights canceled or delayed. Variety heard reports of a one visitor whose flight from Los Angeles to New York turned into 38-hour ordeal.

Thankfully, for a market and industry very used to seeing its fair share of seismic threats, the FAA meltdown appeared to be the only major blip in what many saw as an otherwise upbeat week &#x2014;&#xA0;and perhaps the best AFM in some time.

&#8220;People seem in a happier, positive mood, more so than they've done in the last few years, that's for sure,&#8221; says FilmNation CEO Glen Basner, pointing to a combination of factors. &#8220;They're seeing some box office success. But also, in the last three-four years there's always some mega macro thing that has nothing to do with us that makes their lives very hard. The last thing being massive consolidation of the SVoD players. So I think they understand. It doesn't mean business is easy. It's doesn't mean it's the heyday. But at least people are saying they understand, they have a strategy, and they can enact their strategy &#x2014;&#xA0;and that makes it feel like they're on somewhat solid ground for the first time.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Projects causing a stir on FilmNation's slate include a new as-yet-untitled project from Armando Iannucci starring James McAvoy, Alicia Vikander and Taron Egerton, Patrick Brice's psychosexual horror &#8220;Violet&#8221; starring Lili Reinhart, Danny Ramirez, Suki Waterhouse, Mark Duplass and Lukas Gage, and romantic drama &#8220;Asymmetry&#8221; starring Richard Gere and Diana Silvers, based on Lisa Halliday's acclaimed novel. Helping matters is that the company is coming off the back of recent hits &#8220;The Invite&#8221; and &#8220;I Play Rocky,&#8221; while the stop-motion &#8220;Wildwood&#8221; is also slowly beginning to gather box office steam internationally.

Horror, Basner admits, hasn't been one of FilmNation's mainstays, although &#8220;Violet&#8221; is part of an effort to do more. But across the AFM, where genre has always been a dominant force, it this year was even more present among the packages for sale &#x2014;&#xA0;and understandably given recent successes.

&#8220;There's been an inundation of horror films, which is insane,&#8221; said one buyers, who added that they were &#8220;All in the vein of &#8216;Obsession' and &#8216;Backrooms' and &#8216;Talk to Me' and &#8216;It Follows' &#x2014; it's hilarious.&#8221;

Among the standout titles for sale included Anton's horror &#8220;Ignore It,&#8221; which, much like &#8220;Obsession&#8221; and &#8220;Backrooms,&#8221; marks the first-time feature from a YouTube horror star. Another buyer highlighted Cary Fukunaga's &#8220;excellent&#8221; crime thriller &#8220;Blood on Snow,&#8221; starring Benedict Cumberbatch and Aaron Taylor-Johnson.

But, aside from &#8220;Wet,&#8221; the question still remains of what's actually being bought. While Cannes may have had the joys of a &#8220;bidding war&#8221; for breakout hit &#8220;Club Kid,&#8221; AFM didn't seem to have that one major buzzy project turning heads. A24, in fairness, was shopping its big budget all-star &#8220;Elden Ring&#8221; adaptation, the priciest title on offer, but with Amazon MGM Studios having already bought a number of international pay-1 streaming rights ahead of the market, it was, according to one exec, &#8220;a tricky one.&#8221;

Away from the actual films, there was renewed enthusiasm for the AFM venue, which it decamped to last year after leaving Santa Monica in 2023 and having one &#x2014;&#xA0;thankfully brief &#x2014;&#xA0;stint in Las Vegas.

&#8220;The move to Century City has given the AFM new lifeblood &#x2014;&#xA0;the market is more contained, more convenient and tighter all around,&#8221; said Dylan Leiner, Sony Pictures Classics' executive vice president, acquisitions and production. &#8220;The challenge in the current environment remains for each company to find the films that best suit their approach and their specific mandates, but the titles are there. And not all companies are looking for the same thing.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

And distributors are benefitting from hunting down films that tick their specific boxes &#x2014;&#xA0;and for titles sometimes dismissed by others. Several AFM attendees highlighted black comedy horror &#8220;Buddy,&#8221; which struggled to find a buyer after premiering in Sundance. Months later, Roadside Attractions picked up North American rights, with the film going on to earn $27 million off a $3 million budget &#x2014;&#xA0;Roadside's first to pass $20 million since the pandemic. Another recent hit was &#8220;Wicker,&#8221; the kookie romantic comedy starring Alexander Skarsgard as a wooden sex god &#x2014;&#xA0;which Black Bear helped guide to a domestic haul of more than $20 million &#x2014; and a global pot of $31 million.

&#8220;&#8216;Wicker' is a box office sensation,&#8221; says Basner. &#8220;There are other surprises &#x2014;&#xA0;and that should be the lesson for everybody. Not that you have to do something as as bold as &#8216;Wicker!.&#8221;

Understanding your own distinct market and trusting your creative instincts is the new order of play for buyers looking to find the next unexpected success story.

&#8220;Because the movie may be great, but it may not match up with everybody,&#8221; says Basner. &#8220;People understand how to assign value in a way that lets them go and buy, and maybe even stretch a bit for something that they really respond to. So it's not flashy, like everyone's going crazy. But it's actually really productive and really healthy from a business standpoint.&#8221;

Elsa Keslassy contributed to this article

Kim, 17, lives with her emotionally distant mother and younger brother near Germany's Baltic coast. Their home has a secret space behind a family shrine where strangers come and go. The strangers are Vietnamese migrants. But the mother is not hiding them out of the goodness of her heart. She is The Matriarch ( Die Matriarchin ), and she runs an illegal trafficking operation that brings Vietnamese migrants to Europe.

The sensitive Kim knows that money can't buy her mother's love and recognition. But when an associate of her mother defects to launch a rival operation, the young woman looks to seize the opportunity to prove herself to her mother while navigating her first romance. Can Kim earn the matriarch's respect while staying true to her own moral compass? blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Movies A Man Has Just Retired, But the Government Insists He Is Dead. Welcome to 'When I Existed'! (Exclusive Trailer, Plus Chat With the Directors) Lifestyle Soho House Creator Nick Jones Is Reinventing the Hotel -- Again

The Matriarch is the second feature from writer-director Alison Kuhn. And it is the first German film with an all-Vietnamese lead cast. Thriller meets coming-of-age drama in a movie that explores migration, human trafficking, family dynamics and inherited expectations. The movie premieres in the main competition of the 30th edition of the Tallinn Black Nights Film Festival ( P&#xD6;FF ) in Estonia on Wednesday, Nov. 18.

&#8220;I've always felt the urge to tell a story set within the German-Vietnamese community I was born into and to explore the complex, often unspoken, dynamics of mother-daughter relationships within it,&#8221; says Kuhn. &#8220;Through extensive conversations with members of the diasporic community in Berlin, I encountered recurring emotional patterns: intergenerational tensions, inherited trauma and, to my surprise, the persistent presence of human trafficking &#x2013; a subject that remains largely unnoticed by the wider German public.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The ensemble cast features newcomer Lissy-Marie Nguyen as Kim, Tuyen Do ( Shadow and Bone , Enola Holmes ) as her mother, Viet Pham, Trang Le Hong, Viet Long Hoang and Marlon Minh-Hai Leyck.

Written and directed by Kuhn, The Matriarch was produced by Martin Lehwald and Philipp Goeser for Schiwago Film, with Paula Klossner and Stephan Buske of Tidewater Pictures as co-producers. The movie received support from ZDF &#x2013; Das Kleine Fernsehspiel , MV Filmf&#xF6;rderung, Medienboard Berlin-Brandenburg and Nordmedia. The director of photography is Jan Mayntz, with Christian Zipfel as editor. Pluto Film is handling sales. Watch the trailer for The Matriarch here.

Kuhn has been a director on the series Druck , Watch Me and Shadow Leaks , which reached the Netflix Top 10 in Germany. Her 2025 feature debut Holy Meat , about a village's amateur production of the Passion of Christ that spirals into a blasphemous rave, premiered at Filmfest Munich and won the jury prize at Cinequest in California.

Ahead of the Tallinn world premiere of The Matriarch , Kuhn talked to THR about the research she did for the movie, combining genre filmmaking with family drama and her hopes for more opportunities for Vietnamese creatives in Germany and beyond.

What's different for the creative between directing series and films is that she also writes her features. &#8220;They are stories that have been accompanying me for years, and it was the same in this case,&#8221; she tells THR . Actually, several years ago, Christian Cloos, the commissioning editor of ZDF &#x2013; Das Kleine Fernsehspiel, asked her if there was any story she wanted to tell. &#8220;Well, there is this story about Vietnamese families because half of my family is Vietnamese, and the other half is German,&#8221; Kuhn recalls telling him. She started sharing ideas and stories from her own family. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;But it became too private,&#8221; she tells THR . &#8220;The film was not a thriller back then. It was more a classical social drama, and then I realized I had to find an abstraction. I had to find another perspective.&#8221;

That led her to talk to and about Vietnamese people in Germany, &#8220;and I collected stories, I collected issues that they had, and I started noticing patterns there. There were recurring themes that concerned them. It was a lot about family tensions, intergenerational tensions, responsibility, being pushed into certain roles and sacrificing being close to family members in order to help them grow.&#8221;

And there was one key figure that kept being mentioned. &#8220;Especially the role of mothers, Vietnamese mothers, was something that came up again and again, and so I started creating this character,&#8221; Kuhn shares. &#8220;Another thing that came up in these intense research interviews was the aspect of human trafficking, which is very present in Germany, in Berlin especially. So, I got the idea that I could translate what I wanted to say about mother-daughter relationships into this suspenseful thriller, crime genre without losing the heart of the story. At the heart of it, the film is a mother-daughter story, but it had another guise, or clothing, around it.&#8221;

Audiences outside of Berlin and Germany will find themselves caring about the characters in The Matriarch , and the filmmaker says going deep and local helps with that. &#8220;The more specific you get as a filmmaker, the more you can tell stories about the larger issues of life,&#8221; she explains. &#8220;The emotions underneath all these conflicts that might be culture-related are really universal, and everyone knows them. Everyone wants to be loved by their parents and wants to be appreciated and wants to find their own way. So there are a lot of things there that people from diasporic communities can relate to in particular, but that everyone else also really [understands].&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The human trafficking side of the story in The Matriarch contains some language that will surprise, maybe even shock, audiences. For example, when someone discusses the idea of opening a &#8220;flower shop,&#8221; which turns out to be code for something much more sinister.

&#8220;I always love intense research,&#8221; Kuhn tells THR . &#8220;Before I shoot or write a fictional piece, it's so important for me to know what the actual situation is in order to sort out which parts you need to represent in a realistic, documentary kind of way, and in which parts you can be a little bit more free. In this case, I talked to a lot of investigative journalists and also to the German federal police. They granted me cool insights. I really wanted to understand the structures of these networks and all these operational things. So, for example, flowers, as you mentioned, is a code word for prostitutes. There are a lot of code words that I found very interesting.&#8221;

But she also took creative license and freedom to enhance the story. &#8220;There are fantastic little elements in the film, such as the altar in the living room, which is also the door to a secret room,&#8221; the filmmaker notes. &#8220;This is not something I found in my research. It's based on rooms where these people have to sleep, often under very poor conditions, but there's not this mystery door.&#8221;

Now, how about the cast and how it breaks new ground? &#8220;Yes, we actually are the first German feature film with an all-Vietnamese lead cast,&#8221; Kuhn says with pride. &#8220;It's about time because there is so much talent there that has gone unseen and that has had to put up with small roles &#x2013; one-liners or very stereotypical roles in German movies and series. So it was so cool to have this ensemble, and they all were so appreciative of getting the chance to play parts that have more facets and are more than just one thing on the surface.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid4' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

That said, it was a &#8220;hard&#8221; casting process &#8220;because there are not that many Vietnamese actors and actresses, especially over the age of 45, I would say,&#8221; Kuhn explains. &#8220;There are basically no trained actors and actresses here because of structural and also racist reasons. They did not have the chance to go to acting school and stuff like this. So we had a worldwide casting, especially for the role of the mother and found Tuyen Do, who is based in London.&#8221;

How did she find young Lissy-Marie Nguyen? &#8220;She didn't have any acting experience,&#8221; which you wouldn't believe once you see the film, the director says. &#8220;We found her through her high school. A few years ago, I shot a youth series at a Berlin high school, and they have a huge Vietnamese population there. So our casting director, Stephanie Meile, who is great, wrote to a lot of schools with a lot of Vietnamese families, and we had an open audition process, and she just came in like a force of nature. She has this raw talent. I'm so proud of her because after our shoot, she has continued acting.&#8221;

Kuhn hopes that The Matriarch can help open more doors for Vietnamese talent. &#8220;I have high hopes that people in the industry finally see these talented people,&#8221; she tells THR . &#8220;I hope it will not be the last German film with an all-Vietnamese cast. And I think it's so important to tell different stories from different perspectives. When you talk about movies about human trafficking, there are some in Germany, but most of them are told from an investigator's perspective, and they look down on the people in these networks. But to me, it was very important to tell a story from the perspective of the people involved and show what dramatic situations have pushed them into that place.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid5' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

That social and political context is often chilling. &#8220;You see in some scenes with the mother that, for her, it also has roots going back to the GDR, where contract workers from Vietnam came over and were discriminated against by the state,&#8221; the writer-director notes. &#8220;And they were not allowed to interact with the German community, have children with German people, or learn the language. And after the Wall came down, they were just left alone and had no way to earn money to support their families. Of course, some of these trafficking networks grew out of that.&#8221;

Kuhn didn't run into any opposition or backlash when she pitched The Matriarch as a genre experience. &#8220;But I think there's also a want here of having more female-driven thrillers because there aren't that many,&#8221; she tells THR . &#8220;I looked up a while ago what percentage of thrillers are directed by women, and it was really shockingly low. It seems like this genre has been reserved for men. Also, badass characters have been reserved for men. So, it was very important to me to create this character of the mother who's powerful and really sets the tone.&#8221;

And that comes not only through force but something also through silence or lower-key but effective actions. &#8220;Showing another way of being a leading figure was important because I was asking myself what makes the difference between a female and a male leader of such a network,&#8221; Kuhn says. &#8220;It was very interesting to dive into that. She's not the one with the muscles. She has her people who do that for her. But she has maybe another way of making deals. And she might have another way of putting emotional pressure on people. So that's very interesting about a female boss and often unseen.&#8221;

17 years ago the Academy of Motion Picture Arts & Sciences decided that in order to give their honorary Oscar recipients the time they deserve, and to actually be able to tribute more of the most deserving, they would create a separate ceremony, the Governors Awards and present them at the beginning of the awards-giving season rather than the end. It is now a solid tradition and Sunday night's 17th Annual Governors Awards carried it on in style with a fast paced and emotional ceremony toasting Glenn Close , Ridley Scott , animator Floyd Norman , and the producing team of Christine Vachon and Pamela Toffler of Killer Films who received the Thalberg Award. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories News FAA Ground Stoppage Could Impact One Of Hollywood's Busiest Weekends News Sony's Tom Rothman Hopes It Will Be A 'Brand New Day' For 'Spider-Man' As Studio Launches Major Best Picture Oscar Campaign: "Are You Telling Me There Are 10 Films Better Than This One?"

Coming as it does on a busy awards campaign weekend in November, a time everyone with a foot in the Oscar race still thinks they have a chance for the gold, the Governors Awards has also become a key event to meet and mingle , be seen and heard, and perhaps by appearing in the Ovation Ballroom, the very same place the season will end in March with the Post- Oscars Governors Ball, it might also serve as a reminder to watch their movie. The Academy sent out a tip sheet to press containing approximately 240 expected guest names listed alphabetically from Riz Ahmed to Zendaya, and if you have a film in contention and aren't on it, and happen to be in L.A., you might consider getting another publicist. Studios and distributors spend $150,000 for a table and then make sure all their contenders get a seat at it. Even the evening's perfectly chosen presenters, John Malkovich, Julianne Moore, Mahershala Ali, and Matt Damon happen to all be in performances being campaigned for Oscars this year. Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

But this evening is first and foremost a chance to pay due respect to some genuine legends, journeyman actors, veteran artisans, and significant industry figures who are finally getting Hollywood's most coveted award while they can still appreciate the thought. The Academy knows this and that is why the Governors Awards are always a highlight of awards seasons and a reminder of why we love movies, and those who make them, in the first place. There were standing ovations all around. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The speeches, both from presenters and recipients were about as heartfelt as they come. I had always hoped Glenn Close would win an Oscar, and in fact when she was nominated for The Wife I thought she had it in the bag but she lost in a bit of a surprise to Olivia Colman. Close has been nominated more times, without ever winning, than any other actress alive or dead. Eight times she has been up for the prize and eight times has gone home empty. In a speech where she thanked everyone from casting legend Marion Dougherty to late directors George Roy Hill (who gave her her first film The World According To Garp) and Mike Nichols, to costume designer Ann Roth, she addressed the snubs (I will call them that, she didn't) with grace and dignity. &#8220;As an outsider, I have never worried about winning though some years I thought I might have a chance. I have always felt it's a great honor is to be in the room. Over the past 43 years, I have been in the room 8 times. Part of this extraordinary community. So to be in this room tonight, embraced by the people who have consistently inspired me, whose work has sustained and challenged me, and only made me want to be better, is the greatest honor of all, &#8221; she said.

Presenter Malkovich was warm and witty, especially in pointing out some of the unique ways she has died on screen. &#8220;I think the mark of a great actor is how spectacularly they die on screen. Glenn has had some of the most marvelous character deaths ever captured on film. She was crushed by a fallng chandelier but that's not all. Throughout her illustrious 50 year career she has been decapitated, stung by scorpions, devoured by hungry infected children, she died in several car crashes, got strangled, shot, shot again&#8230;oh and crushed in an elevator which by the way is some of the best crushed-in-an-elevator acting I have ever seen,&#8221; he said to big laughs.

The other big marquee name among the honorees was Scott, a past four-time Oscar nominee, who could not make the flight to L.A. due to doctors orders after a recent medical procedure. He looked and sounded just fine in his pre-taped acceptance which also put the focus of his late brother, and filmmaker, Tony Scott as he shared a clip from his early film Boy And Bicycle featuring Tony as the star. "That was a moment for both of us. Now I'm well past 80, and I've thought about those days and moments. It seems like yesterday. Both of us sharing our first film together that led to 20 years of advertising, five decades of filmmaking (Ridley didn't make his feature film debut until he was 40). I'm flattered and proud to accept this award, but I also accept it for Tony. His great work would certainly have earned this recognition."

Damon was charming in presenting the award and noted his first meeting with Scott who wanted him for The Martian. He said he loved the script but told Ridley he has just done a small but pivotal role in Christopher Nolan's Interstellar in which he played a guy alone and stranded on a planet and desperate to get home. &#8220;So I couldn't follow it up playing a guy alone and stranded on a planet and desperate to get home,&#8221; he said. Scott answered that succinctly with just four words: &#8220;Who gives a shit?&#8221;

The evening opened with Ali presenting an Honorary Oscar to Black animator Floyd Norman who worked at Disney for many years on such classics as Sleeping Beauty, 101 Dalmatians, The Sword In The Stone, Mary Poppins , and on and on. Walt Disney himself mentored Norman after seeing his humorous drawings and signed him up for a major assignment on The Jungle Book which would be Disney's final animated film. &#8220;What a mentor to have, &#8221; Norman said who later landed at Pixar on such films as Toy Story 2, and now in his 90's is still working. He pointed out when he started there were almost no Black animators, but now things are quite different. He also introduced filmmakers who have done a documentary on his life which he said received a bad review from a critic who said he was disappointed because Norman's life lacked &#8216;drama'. &#8220;On this special occasion I want to remind that critic that even movies have happy endings and this special evening is my happy ending.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-articleX","mid-article","btf","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() ; });

The final presentation of the evening was the Irving J. Thalberg Award which went to Vachon and Koffler, the former listing all the genuine movie producing legends from Zanuck to Goldwyn to Mayer to Warner and on and on who won it over Oscar's previous 98 years. Presenters Todd Haynes and Julianne Moore both have a long rich history with Vachon and Koffler and their Killer Films. In fact Haynes noted his controversial Poison was the first film for both he and Vachon, a movie that was attacked on the floors of Congress. Killer Films is waving the flag for true and risky independent filmmaking and the producers said this Thalberg was the first given to the indie sector, as well as the first given to an all-female producing team.

Their legions of movies include Carol, Still Alice, Safe, Far From Heaven, Boys Don't Cry, Past Lives, Hedwig & The Angry Inch (Neil Patrick Harris performed a song from the film during their presentation), and countless others. Moore said she has done 7 films with them and will be starting an 8th next year. "These women have never been driven by profit and even recently admitted to me that no matter how hard they tried to be commercial, they've had to make peace of the fact that they're just not . That doesn't mean that they haven't had commercial success. They are just not driven by it. They are interested in radical voices, queer voices, new voices and personal points of view,&#8221; she said. Haynes said &#8220;they are an example of how the world might actually be if it were run by adults, otherwise known as women."

As they do in their business partnership they took turns expressing their thanks, with Vachon praising many others in the independent community who can take pride in this recognition for indie filmmaking in general. A priceless clip of an old interview with Vachon and Moore was shown in which Vachon talked about a future far off where she might be finally getting her &#8220;Thalberg Award&#8221;, and to which Moore said, &#8220;Yes, and I will present it to you!&#8221; Who knew they were psychic??

Great night all around, and beautifully produced by Jennifer Todd and the Academy which was smart to keep it tight and moving, while still giving all these highly deserving new Oscar recipients a career moment they will never forget.

